"reasoning": "The patient presents with systemic symptoms (fever, weakness) alongside localized dermatological findings: itchy and painful sores on the face that leak a yellow ooze. This clinical picture is highly characteristic of impetigo, a superficial bacterial skin infection known for producing weeping lesions that often form honey-colored or yellow crusts, commonly appearing on the face. Reviewing the training references, cases [4], [6], and [12] directly associate facial/nasal sores with yellow or clear weeping fluid to impetigo. Alternative diagnoses were considered but ruled out: chicken pox typically presents as a widespread vesicular rash rather than localized weeping facial sores; fungal infections and drug reactions manifest as flaking rashes or discolored patches without the distinctive yellow exudate described. The facts firmly establish impetigo as the correct diagnosis.",
"final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]impetigo[/DIAGNOSIS]"
